1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,459
In nineteen sixty,

2
00:00:01,459 --> 00:00:04,946
turning sixteen was the biggest birthday of

3
00:00:04,946 --> 00:00:05,270
all.

4
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:06,819
Bigger than eighteen,

5
00:00:06,819 --> 00:00:08,318
bigger than twenty-one.

6
00:00:08,498 --> 00:00:09,878
Because at sixteen,

7
00:00:09,878 --> 00:00:11,257
in most of America,

8
00:00:11,257 --> 00:00:13,653
you could get a driver's license.

9
00:00:13,833 --> 00:00:16,689
And a driver's license meant one thing.

10
00:00:16,869 --> 00:00:17,484
Freedom.

11
00:00:17,664 --> 00:00:19,361
The freedom to go anywhere,

12
00:00:19,361 --> 00:00:21,623
with anyone, any time you could talk

13
00:00:21,623 --> 00:00:24,073
your father into handing over the keys.

14
00:00:24,422 --> 00:00:27,257
If you turned sixteen in nineteen sixty,

15
00:00:27,257 --> 00:00:29,879
you were born in nineteen forty-four,

16
00:00:29,879 --> 00:00:32,146
during the last full year of the

17
00:00:32,146 --> 00:00:33,351
Second World War.

18
00:00:33,531 --> 00:00:35,608
Your father might have been overseas when

19
00:00:35,608 --> 00:00:36,317
you were born.

20
00:00:36,497 --> 00:00:38,272
You grew up in the boom years

21
00:00:38,272 --> 00:00:39,190
of the fifties.

22
00:00:39,370 --> 00:00:40,806
And now, at sixteen,

23
00:00:40,806 --> 00:00:43,533
the whole country seemed to be waiting

24
00:00:43,533 --> 00:00:44,107
for you.

25
00:00:44,458 --> 00:00:47,142
So let's look at what turning sixteen

26
00:00:47,142 --> 00:00:49,392
in nineteen sixty really meant.

27
00:00:49,572 --> 00:00:51,493
The license, the first car,

28
00:00:51,493 --> 00:00:52,490
the first job,

29
00:00:52,490 --> 00:00:53,913
the first real date,

30
00:00:53,913 --> 00:00:56,546
and all the little freedoms that came

31
00:00:56,546 --> 00:00:57,258
with them.

32
00:00:57,438 --> 00:00:58,641
If that was you,

33
00:00:58,641 --> 00:01:01,048
get ready to feel sixteen again.

34
00:01:01,399 --> 00:01:02,648
In nineteen sixty,

35
00:01:02,648 --> 00:01:05,843
there were about ten million Americans between

36
00:01:05,843 --> 00:01:08,204
the ages of fifteen and seventeen,

37
00:01:08,204 --> 00:01:10,843
and the number was climbing every year

38
00:01:10,843 --> 00:01:12,579
as the baby boom grew up.

39
00:01:12,759 --> 00:01:14,921
Car makers, record companies,

40
00:01:14,921 --> 00:01:18,051
soft drink brands and clothing stores were

41
00:01:18,051 --> 00:01:19,319
all chasing them.

42
00:01:19,499 --> 00:01:22,285
Being sixteen had never mattered so much

43
00:01:22,285 --> 00:01:23,539
to so many people.

44
00:01:23,889 --> 00:01:26,222
It started with driver's education.

45
00:01:26,402 --> 00:01:27,852
By nineteen sixty,

46
00:01:27,852 --> 00:01:30,995
most high schools offered a driver's ed

47
00:01:30,995 --> 00:01:34,057
class, often taught by the gym teacher

48
00:01:34,057 --> 00:01:35,829
or the football coach.

49
00:01:36,009 --> 00:01:37,808
There was classroom time,

50
00:01:37,808 --> 00:01:40,111
with a thick rule book and films

51
00:01:40,111 --> 00:01:41,478
about safe driving.

52
00:01:41,658 --> 00:01:43,121
And then there was the car.

53
00:01:43,471 --> 00:01:46,222
The school's training car was usually a

54
00:01:46,222 --> 00:01:48,762
big sedan donated by a local dealer,

55
00:01:48,762 --> 00:01:51,372
with the school's name painted on the

56
00:01:51,372 --> 00:01:51,725
side.

57
00:01:51,905 --> 00:01:53,714
It had a second brake pedal on

58
00:01:53,714 --> 00:01:54,981
the passenger's side,

59
00:01:54,981 --> 00:01:55,945
for the teacher.

60
00:01:56,125 --> 00:01:58,640
Three or four students rode along,

61
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:00,044
waiting their turn,

62
00:02:00,044 --> 00:02:03,372
and watching their classmates grind the gears

63
00:02:03,372 --> 00:02:05,146
and stall at stop signs.

64
00:02:05,497 --> 00:02:08,219
Those safety films were famous for being

65
00:02:08,219 --> 00:02:08,968
terrifying.

66
00:02:09,148 --> 00:02:12,088
Movies with titles like Signal Thirty and

67
00:02:12,088 --> 00:02:15,028
Mechanized Death showed real car crashes,

68
00:02:15,028 --> 00:02:16,318
with real victims,

69
00:02:16,318 --> 00:02:19,330
to scare teenagers into driving carefully.

70
00:02:19,510 --> 00:02:21,812
Many people who sat through them in

71
00:02:21,812 --> 00:02:24,312
the late fifties and early sixties say

72
00:02:24,312 --> 00:02:26,418
they still remember every frame.

73
00:02:26,768 --> 00:02:28,149
Then came the big day.

74
00:02:28,329 --> 00:02:29,618
The driving test.

75
00:02:29,798 --> 00:02:31,914
You went to the motor vehicle office

76
00:02:31,914 --> 00:02:32,913
with your father,

77
00:02:32,913 --> 00:02:35,146
who was probably more nervous than you

78
00:02:35,146 --> 00:02:35,440
were.

79
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:37,339
You took a written test,

80
00:02:37,339 --> 00:02:38,198
an eye test,

81
00:02:38,198 --> 00:02:40,204
and then sat in the car with

82
00:02:40,204 --> 00:02:42,853
a stern examiner holding a clipboard.

83
00:02:43,033 --> 00:02:45,878
Parallel parking was the part everyone dreaded.

84
00:02:46,228 --> 00:02:48,708
Most cars in nineteen sixty had a

85
00:02:48,708 --> 00:02:51,789
manual transmission, or a column shift on

86
00:02:51,789 --> 00:02:53,217
the steering wheel.

87
00:02:53,397 --> 00:02:55,915
No power steering in many of them,

88
00:02:55,915 --> 00:02:58,656
so parallel parking took real muscle.

89
00:02:58,836 --> 00:03:01,346
Turn signals were standard by then,

90
00:03:01,346 --> 00:03:04,215
but many drivers still used hand signals

91
00:03:04,215 --> 00:03:05,291
out the window.

92
00:03:05,471 --> 00:03:07,263
Arm straight out for left,

93
00:03:07,263 --> 00:03:08,434
arm up for right,

94
00:03:08,434 --> 00:03:09,674
arm down for stop.

95
00:03:10,024 --> 00:03:11,184
When you passed,

96
00:03:11,184 --> 00:03:12,924
you got a paper license,

97
00:03:12,924 --> 00:03:15,027
with no photo in most states.

98
00:03:15,207 --> 00:03:17,272
You folded it into your wallet and

99
00:03:17,272 --> 00:03:18,911
checked it a hundred times.

100
00:03:19,091 --> 00:03:21,528
That night, you asked your father for

101
00:03:21,528 --> 00:03:22,121
the keys.

102
00:03:22,301 --> 00:03:23,428
And the answer,

103
00:03:23,428 --> 00:03:24,855
often, was not yet.

104
00:03:25,035 --> 00:03:26,847
But eventually, he gave in.

105
00:03:27,197 --> 00:03:30,626
Of course, many teenagers had been driving

106
00:03:30,626 --> 00:03:32,259
long before sixteen.

107
00:03:32,439 --> 00:03:35,842
On farms, kids drove tractors and pickup

108
00:03:35,842 --> 00:03:37,884
trucks at ten or twelve.

109
00:03:38,064 --> 00:03:39,029
In the country,

110
00:03:39,029 --> 00:03:41,797
a fourteen-year-old might drive to the feed

111
00:03:41,797 --> 00:03:43,149
store for his father.

112
00:03:43,329 --> 00:03:44,414
In some states,

113
00:03:44,414 --> 00:03:47,093
farm kids could get a special license

114
00:03:47,093 --> 00:03:47,961
at fourteen.

115
00:03:48,141 --> 00:03:50,714
For them, sixteen was just making it

116
00:03:50,714 --> 00:03:51,357
official.

117
00:03:51,707 --> 00:03:55,365
Most sixteen-year-olds in nineteen sixty didn't have

118
00:03:55,365 --> 00:03:56,351
their own car.

119
00:03:56,531 --> 00:03:58,330
They borrowed the family car.

120
00:03:58,510 --> 00:04:00,205
And that came with rules.

121
00:04:00,385 --> 00:04:01,755
Be home by eleven.

122
00:04:01,935 --> 00:04:03,653
No more than three friends.

123
00:04:03,833 --> 00:04:05,354
Don't go over fifty.

124
00:04:05,534 --> 00:04:07,252
Fill it up with gas before you

125
00:04:07,252 --> 00:04:08,054
bring it back.

126
00:04:08,234 --> 00:04:10,013
And don't you dare put a scratch

127
00:04:10,013 --> 00:04:10,347
on it.

128
00:04:10,697 --> 00:04:12,892
The family car might be a big

129
00:04:12,892 --> 00:04:15,465
Chevy Impala, a Ford Fairlane or a

130
00:04:15,465 --> 00:04:16,525
Plymouth Fury.

131
00:04:16,705 --> 00:04:18,154
In nineteen sixty,

132
00:04:18,154 --> 00:04:20,489
those cars had huge tailfins,

133
00:04:20,489 --> 00:04:23,387
lots of chrome and bench seats front

134
00:04:23,387 --> 00:04:24,112
and back.

135
00:04:24,292 --> 00:04:26,503
Teenagers loved the bench seats,

136
00:04:26,503 --> 00:04:29,059
because a girl could slide right over

137
00:04:29,059 --> 00:04:31,270
next to the boy who was driving.

138
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:34,214
Many fathers checked the odometer when the

139
00:04:34,214 --> 00:04:35,079
car came back.

140
00:04:35,259 --> 00:04:37,341
If you'd gone sixty miles to a

141
00:04:37,341 --> 00:04:40,048
drive-in that was only five miles away,

142
00:04:40,048 --> 00:04:41,506
there were questions.

143
00:04:41,686 --> 00:04:44,994
Clever teenagers learned which back roads added

144
00:04:44,994 --> 00:04:46,190
the fewest miles.

145
00:04:46,541 --> 00:04:48,587
The real dream was a car of

146
00:04:48,587 --> 00:04:49,269
your own.

147
00:04:49,449 --> 00:04:51,176
And in nineteen sixty,

148
00:04:51,176 --> 00:04:52,433
it was possible.

149
00:04:52,613 --> 00:04:53,870
An old used car,

150
00:04:53,870 --> 00:04:56,698
like a nineteen forty-nine Ford or a

151
00:04:56,698 --> 00:04:58,348
nineteen fifty Chevy,

152
00:04:58,348 --> 00:05:01,098
might cost a hundred or two hundred

153
00:05:01,098 --> 00:05:01,727
dollars.

154
00:05:01,907 --> 00:05:04,140
A teenage boy with a summer job

155
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:06,446
could save that in a few months.

156
00:05:06,796 --> 00:05:08,909
That first car was usually a wreck.

157
00:05:09,089 --> 00:05:10,911
Rust holes in the floor.

158
00:05:11,091 --> 00:05:13,495
A radio that only got one station.

159
00:05:13,675 --> 00:05:16,159
Tires as bald as an egg.

160
00:05:16,339 --> 00:05:18,146
But to a sixteen-year-old,

161
00:05:18,146 --> 00:05:20,509
it was the most beautiful thing in

162
00:05:20,509 --> 00:05:21,204
the world.

163
00:05:21,384 --> 00:05:23,756
Boys spent every spare hour and every

164
00:05:23,756 --> 00:05:24,589
dollar on it,

165
00:05:24,589 --> 00:05:26,000
washing it, waxing it,

166
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,051
and working on the engine in the

167
00:05:28,051 --> 00:05:28,628
driveway.

168
00:05:28,979 --> 00:05:30,848
Then came the customizing.

169
00:05:31,028 --> 00:05:32,936
Boys lowered their cars,

170
00:05:32,936 --> 00:05:35,082
painted flames on the hood,

171
00:05:35,082 --> 00:05:37,785
and added loud glasspack mufflers.

172
00:05:37,965 --> 00:05:40,532
They hung fuzzy dice from the rearview

173
00:05:40,532 --> 00:05:42,762
mirror, put on a suicide knob for

174
00:05:42,762 --> 00:05:46,005
one-handed steering, and installed a reverb unit

175
00:05:46,005 --> 00:05:46,951
for the radio.

176
00:05:47,131 --> 00:05:49,650
Hot rod magazines showed them how.

177
00:05:49,830 --> 00:05:52,028
Every car told you something about the

178
00:05:52,028 --> 00:05:53,011
boy who drove it.

179
00:05:53,362 --> 00:05:56,969
Gasoline cost about thirty-one cents a gallon

180
00:05:56,969 --> 00:05:58,412
in nineteen sixty.

181
00:05:58,592 --> 00:06:00,651
A dollar's worth would get you through

182
00:06:00,651 --> 00:06:01,843
a weekend of cruising.

183
00:06:02,023 --> 00:06:05,142
Teenagers often pooled their change to buy

184
00:06:05,142 --> 00:06:07,370
gas, a quarter here and a dime

185
00:06:07,370 --> 00:06:07,816
there.

186
00:06:07,996 --> 00:06:09,864
Fill it with two dollars was a

187
00:06:09,864 --> 00:06:11,421
common order at the pump.

188
00:06:11,771 --> 00:06:14,000
Paying for that car meant a job.

189
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:17,144
At sixteen, you could legally work most

190
00:06:17,144 --> 00:06:19,880
jobs, and teenagers were everywhere.

191
00:06:20,060 --> 00:06:21,205
Boys pumped gas,

192
00:06:21,205 --> 00:06:23,639
bagged groceries, flipped burgers,

193
00:06:23,639 --> 00:06:25,572
caddied at the golf course,

194
00:06:25,572 --> 00:06:28,578
and worked on farms and construction crews

195
00:06:28,578 --> 00:06:29,580
in the summer.

196
00:06:29,760 --> 00:06:33,271
Girls babysat, worked at the five-and-dime,

197
00:06:33,271 --> 00:06:36,128
the drugstore or the movie theater,

198
00:06:36,128 --> 00:06:37,597
and waited tables.

199
00:06:37,947 --> 00:06:40,964
The minimum wage in nineteen sixty was

200
00:06:40,964 --> 00:06:42,314
a dollar an hour,

201
00:06:42,314 --> 00:06:44,855
though many teen jobs paid less.

202
00:06:45,035 --> 00:06:47,982
Your first paycheck came in a little

203
00:06:47,982 --> 00:06:49,702
envelope, or as cash.

204
00:06:49,882 --> 00:06:52,416
The first thing many teenagers did was

205
00:06:52,416 --> 00:06:54,217
get a Social Security card,

206
00:06:54,217 --> 00:06:56,218
with their number typed on it,

207
00:06:56,218 --> 00:06:58,753
and they memorized that number for the

208
00:06:58,753 --> 00:07:00,087
rest of their lives.

209
00:07:00,437 --> 00:07:02,143
A job meant more than money.

210
00:07:02,323 --> 00:07:03,985
It meant responsibility,

211
00:07:03,985 --> 00:07:06,201
and being treated like an adult.

212
00:07:06,381 --> 00:07:08,749
A boss who expected you on time.

213
00:07:08,929 --> 00:07:11,367
Customers who didn't care that you were

214
00:07:11,367 --> 00:07:11,867
sixteen.

215
00:07:12,047 --> 00:07:14,147
Many people say they learned more at

216
00:07:14,147 --> 00:07:15,955
their first job than in a whole

217
00:07:15,955 --> 00:07:16,830
year of school.

218
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:19,813
And money meant buying things for yourself,

219
00:07:19,813 --> 00:07:20,976
for the first time.

220
00:07:21,156 --> 00:07:22,958
A new pair of jeans or a

221
00:07:22,958 --> 00:07:25,510
sweater from the department store.

222
00:07:25,690 --> 00:07:27,997
Records, at about a dollar for a

223
00:07:27,997 --> 00:07:28,790
forty-five.

224
00:07:28,970 --> 00:07:30,528
A transistor radio,

225
00:07:30,528 --> 00:07:33,808
which by nineteen sixty was small enough

226
00:07:33,808 --> 00:07:35,367
for a shirt pocket.

227
00:07:35,547 --> 00:07:36,975
Tickets to a concert.

228
00:07:37,155 --> 00:07:38,317
And a class ring,

229
00:07:38,317 --> 00:07:40,983
which cost about thirty dollars and was

230
00:07:40,983 --> 00:07:42,077
a very big deal.

231
00:07:42,428 --> 00:07:44,192
With a car and a little money

232
00:07:44,192 --> 00:07:45,713
came the first real date.

233
00:07:45,893 --> 00:07:47,765
Not a group trip to the soda

234
00:07:47,765 --> 00:07:49,504
fountain, but a real date.

235
00:07:49,684 --> 00:07:51,768
You called her on the family phone,

236
00:07:51,768 --> 00:07:53,852
with your little brother listening.

237
00:07:54,032 --> 00:07:56,064
You asked her father's permission.

238
00:07:56,244 --> 00:07:57,564
On Saturday night,

239
00:07:57,564 --> 00:07:58,957
you washed the car,

240
00:07:58,957 --> 00:08:00,864
put on a clean shirt and a

241
00:08:00,864 --> 00:08:03,430
splash of your father's aftershave.

242
00:08:03,781 --> 00:08:05,492
You picked her up at her front

243
00:08:05,492 --> 00:08:05,778
door.

244
00:08:05,958 --> 00:08:08,241
You talked to her father for five

245
00:08:08,241 --> 00:08:09,487
very long minutes.

246
00:08:09,667 --> 00:08:11,732
Then off to the drive-in,

247
00:08:11,732 --> 00:08:13,962
the movie theater downtown,

248
00:08:13,962 --> 00:08:15,449
or a school dance.

249
00:08:15,629 --> 00:08:17,853
Afterward, a burger and a shake at

250
00:08:17,853 --> 00:08:19,422
the drive-in restaurant,

251
00:08:19,422 --> 00:08:21,973
where a carhop on roller skates brought

252
00:08:21,973 --> 00:08:24,197
the tray right to your car window.

253
00:08:24,547 --> 00:08:26,162
And then the drive home,

254
00:08:26,162 --> 00:08:27,643
as slowly as possible,

255
00:08:27,643 --> 00:08:29,191
with the radio playing.

256
00:08:29,371 --> 00:08:30,764
In nineteen sixty,

257
00:08:30,764 --> 00:08:33,395
the songs were Elvis's It's Now or

258
00:08:33,395 --> 00:08:36,491
Never, Percy Faith's Theme from A Summer

259
00:08:36,491 --> 00:08:39,742
Place, the Everly Brothers' Cathy's Clown,

260
00:08:39,742 --> 00:08:42,142
and Chubby Checker's The Twist.

261
00:08:42,322 --> 00:08:43,891
And she had to be home by

262
00:08:43,891 --> 00:08:44,330
eleven.

263
00:08:44,510 --> 00:08:46,762
Her father was watching from the window.

264
00:08:47,113 --> 00:08:48,560
If things went well,

265
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:50,296
there was a second date,

266
00:08:50,296 --> 00:08:51,164
and a third.

267
00:08:51,344 --> 00:08:53,024
Then maybe going steady,

268
00:08:53,024 --> 00:08:55,054
which meant a class ring on a

269
00:08:55,054 --> 00:08:57,644
chain, or a letter jacket draped over

270
00:08:57,644 --> 00:08:58,624
her shoulders.

271
00:08:58,804 --> 00:09:01,021
Couples went steady for months,

272
00:09:01,021 --> 00:09:01,950
or for years.

273
00:09:02,130 --> 00:09:05,490
And many sixteen-year-olds of nineteen sixty married

274
00:09:05,490 --> 00:09:08,140
their high school sweethearts a few years

275
00:09:08,140 --> 00:09:08,527
later.

276
00:09:08,878 --> 00:09:11,068
On Friday and Saturday nights,

277
00:09:11,068 --> 00:09:13,405
sixteen-year-olds went cruising.

278
00:09:13,585 --> 00:09:15,218
Every town had a strip,

279
00:09:15,218 --> 00:09:17,418
the main street or a highway at

280
00:09:17,418 --> 00:09:18,624
the edge of town.

281
00:09:18,804 --> 00:09:21,785
Teenagers drove slowly up and down,

282
00:09:21,785 --> 00:09:23,148
again and again,

283
00:09:23,148 --> 00:09:25,192
windows down, radios up,

284
00:09:25,192 --> 00:09:26,725
waving to friends,

285
00:09:26,725 --> 00:09:28,173
honking at girls,

286
00:09:28,173 --> 00:09:30,217
and looking for a party.

287
00:09:30,567 --> 00:09:32,679
The drive-in restaurant was the center of

288
00:09:32,679 --> 00:09:33,039
it all.

289
00:09:33,219 --> 00:09:35,222
A&W, Bob's Big Boy,

290
00:09:35,222 --> 00:09:38,172
or a local place with a neon

291
00:09:38,172 --> 00:09:38,699
sign.

292
00:09:38,879 --> 00:09:40,556
Cars circled the lot,

293
00:09:40,556 --> 00:09:41,515
showing off.

294
00:09:41,695 --> 00:09:43,494
Boys revved their engines.

295
00:09:43,674 --> 00:09:46,607
Sometimes, out on a quiet stretch of

296
00:09:46,607 --> 00:09:48,969
road, two cars lined up for a

297
00:09:48,969 --> 00:09:51,331
drag race, which was illegal,

298
00:09:51,331 --> 00:09:52,879
dangerous and very,

299
00:09:52,879 --> 00:09:54,019
very exciting.

300
00:09:54,369 --> 00:09:56,267
For the first time in history,

301
00:09:56,267 --> 00:09:59,240
teenagers could go places without their parents

302
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:01,265
knowing exactly where they were.

303
00:10:01,445 --> 00:10:04,024
That freedom made parents nervous,

304
00:10:04,024 --> 00:10:07,969
and newspapers wrote worried stories about teenagers

305
00:10:07,969 --> 00:10:09,107
and their cars.

306
00:10:09,287 --> 00:10:10,938
But for sixteen-year-olds,

307
00:10:10,938 --> 00:10:12,842
it was the best feeling in the

308
00:10:12,842 --> 00:10:13,223
world.

309
00:10:13,574 --> 00:10:15,954
There were also some dangerous habits.

310
00:10:16,134 --> 00:10:17,831
Nobody wore seat belts,

311
00:10:17,831 --> 00:10:20,487
and many cars didn't even have them.

312
00:10:20,667 --> 00:10:22,808
Teenagers drove too fast,

313
00:10:22,808 --> 00:10:25,891
piled too many friends into the car,

314
00:10:25,891 --> 00:10:27,947
and raced on back roads.

315
00:10:28,127 --> 00:10:30,685
Car crashes were the leading cause of

316
00:10:30,685 --> 00:10:32,689
death for American teenagers.

317
00:10:32,869 --> 00:10:35,463
Almost every high school lost a student

318
00:10:35,463 --> 00:10:36,194
in a crash,

319
00:10:36,194 --> 00:10:37,989
and the whole town mourned.

320
00:10:38,339 --> 00:10:41,726
Turning sixteen in nineteen sixty also meant

321
00:10:41,726 --> 00:10:43,727
noticing the bigger world.

322
00:10:43,907 --> 00:10:47,036
That fall, there was a presidential election

323
00:10:47,036 --> 00:10:49,810
between John Kennedy and Richard Nixon,

324
00:10:49,810 --> 00:10:51,445
and for the first time,

325
00:10:51,445 --> 00:10:54,077
the candidates debated on television.

326
00:10:54,257 --> 00:10:56,428
Teenagers watched with their parents.

327
00:10:56,608 --> 00:10:58,730
Kennedy was young and handsome,

328
00:10:58,730 --> 00:11:01,262
and many teenagers felt like he spoke

329
00:11:01,262 --> 00:11:01,809
to them.

330
00:11:02,159 --> 00:11:04,112
It was also the year of the

331
00:11:04,112 --> 00:11:07,944
Greensboro sit-ins, when young Black college students

332
00:11:07,944 --> 00:11:10,402
sat at a lunch counter and refused

333
00:11:10,402 --> 00:11:11,053
to leave.

334
00:11:11,233 --> 00:11:14,215
Many sixteen-year-olds, Black and white,

335
00:11:14,215 --> 00:11:16,824
watched the news and began to think

336
00:11:16,824 --> 00:11:18,837
about fairness and justice.

337
00:11:19,017 --> 00:11:21,469
Some would join the civil rights movement

338
00:11:21,469 --> 00:11:22,605
within a few years.

339
00:11:22,955 --> 00:11:24,174
And there was the future.

340
00:11:24,354 --> 00:11:26,731
Boys knew that the draft was waiting

341
00:11:26,731 --> 00:11:27,523
at eighteen.

342
00:11:27,703 --> 00:11:30,063
Girls knew they were expected to marry

343
00:11:30,063 --> 00:11:32,237
young, and many would be married by

344
00:11:32,237 --> 00:11:32,672
twenty.

345
00:11:32,852 --> 00:11:34,942
College was becoming more common.

346
00:11:35,122 --> 00:11:38,304
The sixteen-year-olds of nineteen sixty were about

347
00:11:38,304 --> 00:11:40,594
to graduate into a decade that would

348
00:11:40,594 --> 00:11:41,740
change everything.

349
00:11:42,090 --> 00:11:44,769
If you turned sixteen in nineteen sixty,

350
00:11:44,769 --> 00:11:47,047
you're in your early eighties now.

351
00:11:47,227 --> 00:11:49,874
You've driven dozens of cars since then.

352
00:11:50,054 --> 00:11:52,037
But I'd bet you still remember the

353
00:11:52,037 --> 00:11:52,620
first one.

354
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:55,035
The color, the smell of the seats,

355
00:11:55,035 --> 00:11:56,613
the sound of the engine,

356
00:11:56,613 --> 00:11:58,979
and the feeling of driving away from

357
00:11:58,979 --> 00:12:01,280
home for the first time with nobody

358
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:02,332
else in the car.

359
00:12:02,682 --> 00:12:05,735
That's what sixteen meant in nineteen sixty.

360
00:12:05,915 --> 00:12:07,011
Not just a license,

361
00:12:07,011 --> 00:12:08,971
but the start of becoming your own

362
00:12:08,971 --> 00:12:09,375
person.

363
00:12:09,555 --> 00:12:11,006
Making your own money.

364
00:12:11,186 --> 00:12:12,951
Making your own choices.

365
00:12:13,131 --> 00:12:15,836
And learning that freedom always comes with

366
00:12:15,836 --> 00:12:16,591
a few rules,

367
00:12:16,591 --> 00:12:18,541
usually written by your father.

368
00:12:18,891 --> 00:12:20,783
So tell us in the comments.

369
00:12:20,963 --> 00:12:22,392
What was your first car,

370
00:12:22,392 --> 00:12:24,058
and how much did you pay for

371
00:12:24,058 --> 00:12:24,237
it?

372
00:12:24,417 --> 00:12:25,968
Where did you go on your first

373
00:12:25,968 --> 00:12:26,588
drive alone?

374
00:12:26,768 --> 00:12:28,057
What was your first job?

375
00:12:28,237 --> 00:12:29,653
We read every comment.

376
00:12:29,833 --> 00:12:32,245
Next time, we're looking at what every

377
00:12:32,245 --> 00:12:34,911
American family ate for dinner in nineteen

378
00:12:34,911 --> 00:12:35,673
fifty-eight.

379
00:12:35,853 --> 00:12:37,593
Thank you for spending this time with

380
00:12:37,593 --> 00:12:37,734
me.
